msgid "The older way: the `&&` chain"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/06-objects.md:409
msgid "Older rules check every level with `&&`. It stops at the first falsy value and returns it (lecture 3):"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/06-objects.md:422
msgid "It works, but the path is written again and again, and `&&` also stops at `0` and `\"\"` - values that can be real data."
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/06-objects.md:425
msgid "Optional chaining `?.` and `??` (ES2020)"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/06-objects.md:427
msgid "`a?.b` means: \"if `a` is `undefined` or `null`, stop here and give `undefined`; otherwise read `a.b`\". It works with brackets and calls too: `a?.[key]`, `f?.()`."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
